1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,026
July nineteen fifty-two.

2
00:00:02,026 --> 00:00:04,496
The night sky over Washington, D.C.

3
00:00:04,496 --> 00:00:13,033
Over the White House, the Capitol, and the most heavily defended airspace on Earth, something is showing up on the radar.

4
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:14,420
Not one blip.

5
00:00:14,420 --> 00:00:14,845
Seven.

6
00:00:14,845 --> 00:00:20,087
Hovering, drifting, then jumping away faster than any aircraft of the day.

7
00:00:20,087 --> 00:00:23,699
Controllers swear the scopes are working perfectly.

8
00:00:24,166 --> 00:00:25,472
Jets are scrambled.

9
00:00:25,472 --> 00:00:29,046
One pilot radios that the lights are all around him.

10
00:00:29,046 --> 00:00:35,233
A week later, the Air Force holds the biggest press conference since the Second World War.

11
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:38,916
Newspapers across America print the story.

12
00:00:38,916 --> 00:00:41,520
Then the Pentagon gives an answer.

13
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:42,133
Hot air.

14
00:00:42,133 --> 00:00:43,971
A temperature inversion.

15
00:00:43,971 --> 00:00:44,890
Case closed.

16
00:00:44,890 --> 00:00:45,733
But was it?

17
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:47,676
I'm your investigator.

18
00:00:47,676 --> 00:00:54,720
Tonight we open the radar room tapes, weigh the evidence against the alien theory, and deliver a verdict.

19
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:56,666
Let's start at the beginning.

20
00:01:01,133 --> 00:01:03,128
Summer, nineteen fifty-two.

21
00:01:03,128 --> 00:01:05,345
A heat wave grips the capital.

22
00:01:05,345 --> 00:01:10,666
America is deep in the Cold War, and the country is jumpy about the sky.

23
00:01:11,133 --> 00:01:14,863
Flying saucers are already a national obsession.

24
00:01:14,863 --> 00:01:19,525
In April, Life magazine asks if we have visitors from space.

25
00:01:19,525 --> 00:01:23,333
By July, reports are pouring in at a record pace.

26
00:01:23,799 --> 00:01:30,872
In the first half of the year, the Air Force logs more new reports than in the previous four years combined.

27
00:01:30,872 --> 00:01:33,033
Project Blue Book is overwhelmed.

28
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:39,333
And the capital's air traffic control center is about to have the strangest shift of its career.

29
00:01:39,799 --> 00:01:40,462
Item one.

30
00:01:40,462 --> 00:01:42,818
Saturday night, July nineteenth.

31
00:01:42,818 --> 00:01:52,166
Just before midnight, controller Edward Nugent sees seven unidentified targets about fifteen miles south-southwest of the city.

32
00:01:52,633 --> 00:01:57,206
They drift slowly, then lurch ahead at speeds that seem impossible.

33
00:01:57,206 --> 00:01:59,731
His senior controller checks the set.

34
00:01:59,731 --> 00:02:01,233
It's working normally.

35
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:05,059
Radar at the national airport sees them too.

36
00:02:05,059 --> 00:02:07,808
And radar at Andrews Air Force Base.

37
00:02:07,808 --> 00:02:13,000
Three separate sets, three separate rooms, the same unknown objects.

38
00:02:13,466 --> 00:02:20,079
At Andrews, an airman looks up and sees an orange ball with a tail, streaking across the sky.

39
00:02:20,079 --> 00:02:23,066
This is no longer just a blip on a screen.

40
00:02:23,533 --> 00:02:30,116
A Capital Airlines pilot reports six white lights shooting past his plane over fourteen minutes.

41
00:02:30,116 --> 00:02:33,133
Tower controllers see a hovering light, too.

42
00:02:33,599 --> 00:02:36,708
Then the moment that chills the controllers.

43
00:02:36,708 --> 00:02:43,633
An object sits right over a radio beacon, and on all three radars it vanishes at the same instant.

44
00:02:44,099 --> 00:02:48,857
Around three in the morning, two F-94 jets arrive from Delaware.

45
00:02:48,857 --> 00:02:51,683
As they approach, the blips disappear.

46
00:02:51,683 --> 00:02:56,366
When the jets run low on fuel and leave, the targets come back.

47
00:02:56,833 --> 00:03:00,980
The last target fades around five-thirty, with the sunrise.

48
00:03:00,980 --> 00:03:05,900
Nobody in the radar room has any doubt something strange has happened.

49
00:03:06,366 --> 00:03:07,006
Item two.

50
00:03:07,006 --> 00:03:15,033
The man in charge of Project Blue Book, Captain Edward Ruppelt, learns about all of it from the Monday headlines.

51
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:18,357
He tries to get a staff car and fly back.

52
00:03:18,357 --> 00:03:18,984
He can't.

53
00:03:18,984 --> 00:03:24,699
For the next week, the official investigation is a phone call away from the story.

54
00:03:25,166 --> 00:03:28,161
One officer already suggests the answer.

55
00:03:28,161 --> 00:03:32,205
Weather can bend radar beams and create false targets.

56
00:03:32,205 --> 00:03:33,628
Remember that idea.

57
00:03:33,628 --> 00:03:35,800
It becomes the official line.

58
00:03:36,266 --> 00:03:37,097
Item three.

59
00:03:37,097 --> 00:03:39,212
Saturday, July twenty-sixth.

60
00:03:39,212 --> 00:03:40,572
A second Saturday.

61
00:03:40,572 --> 00:03:41,629
A second flap.

62
00:03:41,629 --> 00:03:44,500
This time, the whole city is watching.

63
00:03:44,966 --> 00:03:49,932
At eight-fifteen in the evening, a National Airlines crew sees lights.

64
00:03:49,932 --> 00:03:53,266
Radar picks the same targets up almost at once.

65
00:03:53,733 --> 00:03:56,705
Blue Book's press officer is at the airport.

66
00:03:56,705 --> 00:03:59,339
Reporters are crowding the radar rooms.

67
00:03:59,339 --> 00:04:04,000
Some of the targets are said to move at seven thousand miles an hour.

68
00:04:04,466 --> 00:04:07,497
At eleven-thirty, jets are scrambled again.

69
00:04:07,497 --> 00:04:09,824
Captain John McHugo sees nothing.

70
00:04:09,824 --> 00:04:14,900
But Lieutenant William Patterson sees four white glows, and gives chase.

71
00:04:15,366 --> 00:04:19,795
His words, as reported: I see them now, and they're all around me.

72
00:04:19,795 --> 00:04:21,473
He turns, they slip away.

73
00:04:21,473 --> 00:04:25,500
He calls off the chase when the lights seem to surround him.

74
00:04:25,966 --> 00:04:32,682
Two experienced officers, Major Dewey Fournet and Navy Lieutenant John Holcomb, watch the scopes.

75
00:04:32,682 --> 00:04:35,312
They judge the returns good and solid.

76
00:04:35,312 --> 00:04:38,566
A weak inversion, they say, can't explain them.

77
00:04:39,033 --> 00:04:40,956
The lights end at sunrise.

78
00:04:40,956 --> 00:04:41,399
Again.

79
00:04:41,866 --> 00:04:42,575
Item four.

80
00:04:42,575 --> 00:04:45,126
The White House is paying attention.

81
00:04:45,126 --> 00:04:50,300
President Truman's air aide asks the Air Force what on Earth is going on.

82
00:04:50,766 --> 00:04:54,259
On July twenty-ninth, two generals face the press.

83
00:04:54,259 --> 00:04:58,800
The largest Pentagon press conference since the Second World War.

84
00:04:59,266 --> 00:05:06,620
General John Samford says the Air Force has had credible observers report relatively incredible things.

85
00:05:06,620 --> 00:05:10,833
Then he says the answer is probably temperature inversions.

86
00:05:11,300 --> 00:05:14,978
An inversion is a layer of warm air over cool air.

87
00:05:14,978 --> 00:05:19,981
It can bend a radar beam back to the ground and show a ghost target.

88
00:05:19,981 --> 00:05:21,379
It's a real effect.

89
00:05:21,379 --> 00:05:23,733
The question is whether it fits.

90
00:05:24,199 --> 00:05:24,891
Item five.

91
00:05:24,891 --> 00:05:26,413
The cross-examination.

92
00:05:26,413 --> 00:05:31,600
Let's weigh the evidence for each explanation, starting with the inversion.

93
00:05:32,066 --> 00:05:38,985
For the inversion: a government weather study finds one in almost every one of the nights in question.

94
00:05:38,985 --> 00:05:43,666
A famous astronomer, Donald Menzel, and sceptic Philip Klass back it.

95
00:05:44,133 --> 00:05:51,091
For the sceptics, one radar target was traced to a steamboat on the Potomac, heading for Mount Vernon.

96
00:05:51,091 --> 00:05:55,866
Many of the visual sightings were stars, meteors, and aircraft lights.

97
00:05:56,333 --> 00:06:03,964
Klass adds that early radar was prone to false targets, and that later digital filters wiped many of them away.

98
00:06:03,964 --> 00:06:07,058
He called the controllers two dumb operators.

99
00:06:07,058 --> 00:06:08,433
That's a harsh take.

100
00:06:08,899 --> 00:06:11,579
Against it: the controllers were not new.

101
00:06:11,579 --> 00:06:16,675
Some had years of experience, and they said these returns were hard and sharp.

102
00:06:16,675 --> 00:06:18,766
Not the fuzzy clutter they knew.

103
00:06:19,233 --> 00:06:22,847
And the radar matched what people saw with their eyes.

104
00:06:22,847 --> 00:06:27,733
Howard Cocklin, decades later: I saw it on the screen and out the window.

105
00:06:28,199 --> 00:06:30,079
Three independent radars.

106
00:06:30,079 --> 00:06:31,432
Pilots in the air.

107
00:06:31,432 --> 00:06:33,236
Witnesses on the ground.

108
00:06:33,236 --> 00:06:35,492
An inversion can fool one set.

109
00:06:35,492 --> 00:06:38,800
Fooling everything at once is a harder sell.

110
00:06:39,266 --> 00:06:42,725
Even a later university study could not settle it.

111
00:06:42,725 --> 00:06:47,291
A physicist said witnesses still disputed the weather explanation.

112
00:06:47,291 --> 00:06:50,266
Jacques Vallée called the case rather poor.

113
00:06:50,733 --> 00:06:51,378
Item six.

114
00:06:51,378 --> 00:06:52,597
The alien theory.

115
00:06:52,597 --> 00:06:58,833
Visitors from another world, scouting the capital of the most powerful nation on Earth.

116
00:06:59,300 --> 00:07:02,708
Why would they come two weeks in a row, then vanish?

117
00:07:02,708 --> 00:07:07,166
Why show up only as lights and radar blips, never land, never speak?

118
00:07:07,633 --> 00:07:10,386
What the evidence does show is a loop.

119
00:07:10,386 --> 00:07:12,269
Headlines drove sightings.

120
00:07:12,269 --> 00:07:14,153
Sightings drove headlines.

121
00:07:14,153 --> 00:07:20,166
Sixteen thousand newspaper items ran in about a hundred and fifty papers that year.

122
00:07:20,633 --> 00:07:27,800
Once the story was in every paper, every star became a saucer, and every controller was watching for one.

123
00:07:28,266 --> 00:07:31,204
And that's where the story takes a darker turn.

124
00:07:31,204 --> 00:07:35,766
Because the government was just as worried about the panic as the lights.

125
00:07:36,233 --> 00:07:37,108
Item seven.

126
00:07:37,108 --> 00:07:39,497
September, nineteen fifty-two.

127
00:07:39,497 --> 00:07:48,333
A CIA memo warns that floods of saucer reports could jam military channels, and might even spark mass hysteria.

128
00:07:48,800 --> 00:07:54,107
In January nineteen fifty-three, a panel of scientists meets for four days.

129
00:07:54,107 --> 00:07:58,000
They review the best cases, and watch the famous films.

130
00:07:58,466 --> 00:08:04,182
Their conclusion: nothing threatens national security, but the public interest does.

131
00:08:04,182 --> 00:08:04,794
The cure?

132
00:08:04,794 --> 00:08:08,333
Debunk the sightings, and strip the aura of mystery.

133
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:10,016
Blue Book shifts.

134
00:08:10,016 --> 00:08:15,454
It starts explaining cases away, and it stops publicising the ones it can't.

135
00:08:15,454 --> 00:08:20,033
The Washington flap changed how the government talks about UFOs.

136
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:21,264
Item eight.

137
00:08:21,264 --> 00:08:22,237
The scorecard.

138
00:08:22,237 --> 00:08:26,199
Three claims, tested against what we can actually verify.

139
00:08:26,666 --> 00:08:27,462
Claim one.

140
00:08:27,462 --> 00:08:29,530
Radar saw unknown targets.

141
00:08:29,530 --> 00:08:30,246
Verified.

142
00:08:30,246 --> 00:08:36,133
Multiple operators, multiple radars, multiple nights, in official records.

143
00:08:36,600 --> 00:08:37,352
Claim two.

144
00:08:37,352 --> 00:08:41,342
Pilots and controllers saw lights in the same places.

145
00:08:41,342 --> 00:08:42,547
Partly verified.

146
00:08:42,547 --> 00:08:47,666
Real reports exist, but many lights match stars, meteors and planes.

147
00:08:48,133 --> 00:08:48,991
Claim three.

148
00:08:48,991 --> 00:08:51,352
An inversion explains everything.

149
00:08:51,352 --> 00:08:52,139
Not proven.

150
00:08:52,139 --> 00:08:57,433
It likely explains many targets, but no study ever matched every sighting.

151
00:08:57,899 --> 00:08:58,746
Claim four.

152
00:08:58,746 --> 00:08:59,824
Aliens did it.

153
00:08:59,824 --> 00:09:00,747
Unsupported.

154
00:09:00,747 --> 00:09:04,826
There is no physical evidence, no photo, no wreckage.

155
00:09:04,826 --> 00:09:07,366
Only lights, scopes, and stories.

156
00:09:07,833 --> 00:09:08,617
The verdict.

157
00:09:08,617 --> 00:09:10,708
Unresolved, but leaning weather.

158
00:09:10,708 --> 00:09:17,700
The inversion is a strong candidate, but the people in the room never accepted it, and nobody can prove it.

159
00:09:18,166 --> 00:09:27,533
What we can say for certain: the Air Force and the CIA took the sky seriously, the press took it more seriously, and the public never forgot.

160
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,759
Today, radar is digital and filtered.

161
00:09:30,759 --> 00:09:37,100
And the odd thing is, nobody has reported a night like July nineteen fifty-two since.

162
00:09:37,566 --> 00:09:40,873
If the lights were weather, the weather has never repeated it.

163
00:09:40,873 --> 00:09:43,966
If they were something else, they haven't returned either.

164
00:09:44,433 --> 00:09:47,664
So what do you think was in the sky over Washington?

165
00:09:47,664 --> 00:09:50,275
Weather, or something we still can't name?

166
00:09:50,275 --> 00:09:51,766
Tell us in the comments.

167
00:09:52,233 --> 00:09:59,833
Next case: a night in Iran, nineteen seventy-six, when two jets chased a light, and one of them lost every system.

168
00:10:00,299 --> 00:10:02,299
Subscribe for the next case file.
